From f988bd69d57ff865799629b456853c9b1a9266ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:04:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] --debug: #line directives, so a debugger steps the Goose
 source

Under --debug every line of a function's C is preceded by a #line naming
the Goose statement it came from: the statement's line, or the function
declaration's line for the prologue. One directive per C line rather than
per statement, because after `#line N` the following C lines would count
on as N+1, N+2 and be charged to the wrong Goose lines. File names are
absolute and normalized, so a debugger finds them from wherever it runs.

Each function ends with a marker that the driver, once the C is
assembled, replaces with a #line back to the .c file itself at its true
line, so the runtime and the helpers that follow are not charged to
Goose. Without --debug nothing changes. No #line 0 is ever written
(C17 6.10.4 wants 1 to 2147483647).

Test, on a program whose main calls a function at line 9:

    goose build --debug -o prog prog.goose
    lldb --batch -o 'b prog.goose:9' -o run -o bt ./prog

The breakpoint binds to prog.goose:9 and the backtrace names .goose
files and lines. goose run --debug (TinyCC) compiles the directives too.
